<p class="ltx_p">where in (<a href="./23.8#E4" title="(23.8.4) ‣ §23.8(ii) Series of Cosecants and Cotangents ‣ §23.8 Trigonometric Series and Products ‣ Weierstrass Elliptic Functions ‣ Chapter 23 Weierstrass Elliptic and Modular Functions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23.8.4</span></a>) the terms in <math class="ltx_Math" altimg="m30.png" altimg-height="13px" altimg-valign="-2px" altimg-width="17px" alttext="n" display="inline"><mi href="./23.1#t1.r2" title="integer">n</mi></math> and <math class="ltx_Math" altimg="m5.png" altimg-height="18px" altimg-valign="-4px" altimg-width="33px" alttext="-n" display="inline"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi href="./23.1#t1.r2" title="integer">n</mi></mrow></math> are to be bracketed
together (the <em class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Eisenstein convention</em>
or <em class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">principal value</em>:
see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet">Weil (<a href="./bib/W#bib2387" title="Elliptic Functions According to Eisenstein and Kronecker" class="ltx_ref">1999</a>, p. 6)</cite> or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet">Walker (<a href="./bib/W#bib2359" title="Elliptic Functions. A Constructive Approach" class="ltx_ref">1996</a>, p. 3)</cite>).
</p>
